# Probability Arena — CRYPTO-COVERAGE-REPAIR-001 provider-free reconciliation.
# Revisits already-persisted tokens whose survival horizons have matured so
# 6h/24h outcomes can populate from evidence already on disk. Reads and writes
# ONLY our own lifecycle/outcome rows. Zero external calls, zero provider
# budget, no discovery scan, no cohort, no arming, no wallets, no keys, no
# swaps, no signing, no orders, no execution of any kind.
# A complete no-op while ENABLE_CRYPTO_TAPE_RECONCILER is false (set in .env).
# NOT auto-installed. See the .timer file for install commands.

[Unit]
# Deliberately no After=network-online.target: this unit makes zero external
# calls, and declaring a network dependency would invite a future reader to
# believe otherwise.
Description=Probability Arena crypto survival reconciliation (provider-free)

[Service]
Type=oneshot
WorkingDirectory=%h/projects/probability-arena
EnvironmentFile=%h/projects/probability-arena/.env
ExecStart=%h/projects/probability-arena/.venv/bin/python -m app.cli crypto-tape-reconcile
NoNewPrivileges=true
# The pass commits in bounded batches (each individual write-lock hold is a
# small fraction of a second, not the whole pass — see crypto_tape.py's
# RECONCILE_BATCH_SIZE comment), but this is a SQLite writer on a host with a
# 5-minute MarketOps timer, a continuously-writing watcher, and a documented
# lock-contention history, and a competing writer's worst-case WAIT still
# tracks this pass's WALL TIME, not the per-batch hold (measured: SQLite's
# busy handler loses the lock race against ~80 back-to-back short write
# transactions almost as easily as against one long one — see NEW-H2 in
# crypto_tape.py and app/config.py). Yield CPU and IO so reconciliation never
# wins a scheduling contest against the live lanes.
Nice=10
CPUWeight=20
IOWeight=20
# NOT a licence to run long. The scheduled pass stops at an internal
# wall-clock deadline (default 20s = crypto_tape_reconciler_max_duration_seconds)
# plus at most one in-flight batch; that combined figure — NOT the per-batch
# write-lock hold — is the honest bound on how long a competing writer can be
# blocked, and at the shipped default it is >=67% of the 30s busy_timeout.
# MEDIUM fix (third re-review, M2): `run_scheduled_reconciliation` never
# overrides `max_lock_attempts`/`lock_retry_seconds`, so DB_LOCKED_MAX_ATTEMPTS
# (3) / DB_LOCKED_RETRY_SECONDS (3.0s) apply, and the deadline is only
# checked BETWEEN batches, not during one.
#
# RE-DERIVED, CRYPTO-RECONCILER-LOCK-WAIT-BUDGET-001 B1 (independent review).
# The previous derivation here — "~20s (deadline) + ~96s (one in-flight batch)
# + ~96s (finalize) = ~212s" — was the OVERSHOOT-1.0 figure. It is accurate for
# an IDLE host and was never reconciled with the load-dependent overshoot
# (measured 1.01x on EVO idle, 5.80x on a dev Mac at load average 5-6), and it
# also predated the lock-wait budget, which replaced the batch loop's
# 30s-per-acquisition wait with a deadline-derived share (5s at the shipped
# 20s deadline). Current derivation, at the shipped
# RECONCILE_LOCK_WAIT_STATEMENT_OVERSHOOT of 2.0:
#
#   deadline                                                        20s
#   one in-flight batch's ladder  3 x (2 blocking pts x 2.0) x 5s
#                                 + 2 x 3.0s retry sleeps          = 66s
#   finalize  1 attempt (RECONCILE_FINALIZE_MAX_LOCK_ATTEMPTS) at
#             the inherited 30s busy timeout x 2.0                 = 60s
#   ------------------------------------------------------------------
#   honest worst-case bound at overshoot 2.0                       ~146s
#
# The finalize is ONE attempt precisely because of this line: a 3-attempt
# ladder at the inherited 30s timeout costs ~186s at overshoot 2.0 and ~528s at
# the measured 5.80x. At 5.80x the whole-unit figure above becomes ~374s.
#
# CRYPTO-RECONCILER-GUARDED-TIMER-001 — RECONCILED, NOT LEFT DANGLING. The
# previous value (300s) was EXCEEDED by that ~374s ceiling, and the milestone
# that noticed it left the discrepancy documented rather than closed, on the
# grounds that the timer was attended-only. It is not attended-only any more:
# Eric approved a RECURRING, unattended, 6-hourly timer. Same model, load
# factor L applied to every lock-dependent term:
#
#   deadline (wall clock, no lock involved)                     20s
#   one in-flight batch ladder  3 attempts x (2 blocking points
#     x L) x 5s derived budget  + 2 x 3.0s retry sleeps     = 30L + 6
#   finalize  1 attempt (RECONCILE_FINALIZE_MAX_LOCK_ATTEMPTS)
#     at the inherited 30s busy timeout x L                 = 30L
#   ---------------------------------------------------------------
#   whole-unit ceiling                                      = 26 + 60L
#
#     L = 1.01x  (EVO, idle, measured)                          ~87s
#     L = 2.0x   (the shipped chosen overshoot constant)       ~146s
#     L = 5.80x  (dev Mac at load average 5-6, measured)       ~374s
#
# THE LOAD FACTOR AT WHICH THIS VALUE IS EXCEEDED IS L = 6.57, and it is
# written down here so the next reader does not have to re-derive it:
# 26 + 60L > 420  <=>  L > 394/60 = 6.566...; at L = 6.6 the ceiling is 422s,
# i.e. 2s past the chosen 420s. The gap between the worst measured L (5.80) and
# the crossing L (6.57) is ~13% of load headroom, NOT a safety margin — L is
# unbounded above. THE SIGNATURE OF AN OVERRUN IS THE ORPHANED `running` ROW
# TALLY (`lock_wait_run_row_orphaned()`, see below): a SIGKILL mid-finalize is
# the ONLY thing that produces a run row at status='running' with no
# `config.write_coordination`, so a rising orphan count IS an L>6.57 report
# from the host, and it is how an exceedance is noticed rather than assumed.
#
# 7min (420s) covers the worst L this repo has ever MEASURED, with ~12%
# headroom. That headroom is NOT a guarantee and must not be quoted as one: L
# tracks host load and is unbounded above, so no constant can bound it — which
# is exactly why the recurring timer's precondition is OBSERVED non-exceedance
# of the per-pass `wall_time_model_ms`, and why `wall_time_model_exceeded=true`
# is one of the post-run REVIEW triggers that feeds the auto-disable gate.
#
# WHAT HAPPENS WHEN IT IS HIT ANYWAY (unchanged, and non-corrupting): systemd
# SIGKILLs the pass. Batches committed before that moment stay durable; if the
# kill lands mid-finalize the run row is left at status='running' with no
# `config.write_coordination`. That orphan is already CLASSIFIED, not
# mysterious: `lock_wait_run_row_orphaned()` names exactly that signature, and
# the counted-passes analysis excludes and separately tallies it. Nothing
# reconciles those rows; the tokens themselves return to the backlog.
#
# See "Guarded recurring reconciler timer", "Recurring-timer preconditions"
# and "TimeoutStartSec vs the finalize ladder" in docs/EVO_X2_RUNBOOK.md.
TimeoutStartSec=7min

# CRYPTO-RECONCILER-GUARDED-TIMER-001 — WHAT GUARDS THIS UNIT, since a
# recurring unattended writer needs more than a deadline:
#   * PRE-FLIGHT SKIP — the pass refuses to start when MarketOps is unhealthy
#     (`_reconciliation_should_abort`, reused) or when the health latch is
#     tripped. Both are typed, recorded, non-zero-exit outcomes; a skipped run
#     is data, never silence.
#   * PER-RUN MARK — a batch-phase lock wait >=2000ms marks the run for
#     review. It disables nothing on its own; it is what makes "escalating or
#     repeated" mean anything BELOW the 5000ms cliff, by feeding the rolling
#     3-of-4 review rule.
#   * PER-RUN ABORT — a batch-phase lock wait >=5000ms is recorded as a
#     warning and the run continues; >=7000ms stops the ladder through the
#     existing `stop_reason=lock_wait_budget` path (prior batches stay
#     durable). All three numbers are CHOSEN operational policy, not
#     measurements. THE LOCK-WAIT BUDGET CAPS THE WAIT; THESE LINES CAP THE
#     LADDER — a wait is classified after it completes, so 7000ms is not a
#     ceiling on any single wait and must never be read as one.
#   * ROLLING AUTO-DISABLE — repeated severe waits, sustained contention, a
#     MarketOps regression, or sustained review pressure trip a LATCH that
#     blocks further scheduled runs until a human clears it with
#     `crypto-reconciler-health --clear --operator <name>`. Nothing clears it
#     automatically. A tripped gate also exits non-zero, so this unit goes red.
#     NEVER `rm` the health state file — a deleted latch reads exactly like a
#     host that never tripped. See the runbook.

[Install]
WantedBy=default.target
